A class can declare one or more **type parameters** -- conventionally single uppercase letters like `T`, `E`, `K`, `V` -- in angle brackets after its name, and use that type parameter anywhere a concrete type would otherwise appear inside the class:

```java
public class Box<T> {
    private T contents;

    public void set(T contents) {
        this.contents = contents;
    }

    public T get() {
        return contents;
    }
}

Box<String> stringBox = new Box<>();
stringBox.set("hello");
String value = stringBox.get();   // no cast needed -- the compiler already knows get() returns a String here
```

`Box<String>` and `Box<Integer>` behave, from the caller's point of view, like two specialized versions of `Box` -- one that only accepts and returns `String`, one that only accepts and returns `Integer` -- with the compiler enforcing that specialization at every call site and eliminating the need for a manual cast on `get()`.

Methods can be generic too, independently of whether their enclosing class is: `public static <T> T firstOf(List<T> list) { return list.get(0); }` declares its own type parameter `T`, inferred from whatever list is actually passed in at each call site. A **bounded type parameter**, `<T extends Comparable<T>>`, restricts `T` to only types that implement `Comparable<T>` -- this is how you write a generic method that needs to call `.compareTo()` on its elements (a max-finder, a sort routine) while still working for any element type that actually supports comparison, rather than being hardcoded to one specific type or accepting types that would fail at the `.compareTo()` call.

Two restrictions that otherwise seem arbitrary become obvious once you know about erasure. First, you cannot write `new T[10]` inside a generic class -- creating an array in Java requires the JVM to know the array's actual element type at run time (arrays, unlike generic collections, do track their element type at run time, for array-store checking), but after erasure, `T` simply doesn't exist as a real type anymore at that point; there's nothing concrete for `new T[10]` to actually allocate an array *of*. (The standard workaround, when a generic class genuinely needs an array internally, is to create an `Object[]` and cast it, which is exactly what `ArrayList`'s own internal implementation does.)

Second, you cannot write `if (obj instanceof List<String>)` -- `instanceof` needs to check a real run-time type, and after erasure, there's no way to ask 'is this specifically a `List` of `String`s' at run time, because that distinction never survives to run time at all; the compiler only allows the unparameterized check, `obj instanceof List`, which asks 'is this a `List` of *something*' and nothing more specific.

```java
public class Box<T> {
    // private T[] items = new T[10];   // compile error -- cannot create a generic array
    private Object[] items = new Object[10];   // the standard erasure workaround

    @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
    public T get(int i) {
        return (T) items[i];   // compiler-inserted-style cast, marked unchecked since it can't verify T at run time
    }
}
```

