A class implements `Comparable<T>` to declare that it has one intrinsic, 'natural' way to be ordered -- baked into the class itself, via a single method, `compareTo(T other)`. Its contract: return a negative number if `this` should sort *before* `other`, a positive number if `this` should sort *after* `other`, and zero if they're considered equal for ordering purposes. The exact magnitude of the returned number is never meaningful, only its **sign** -- returning `-1` versus `-1000` makes no difference to any sorting code; both simply mean 'comes before.'

```java
public class Employee implements Comparable<Employee> {
    private String name;
    private double salary;

    public Employee(String name, double salary) {
        this.name = name;
        this.salary = salary;
    }

    @Override
    public int compareTo(Employee other) {
        return Double.compare(this.salary, other.salary);   // natural order: ascending by salary
    }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return name + "(" + salary + ")";
    }
}

List<Employee> employees = new ArrayList<>(List.of(
    new Employee("Amy", 75000), new Employee("Ben", 55000), new Employee("Cara", 90000)
));
Collections.sort(employees);
System.out.println(employees);   // [Ben(55000.0), Amy(75000.0), Cara(90000.0)]
```

`Collections.sort(employees)` works with no additional argument at all, precisely because `Employee` declared its own natural order by implementing `Comparable`. Note the use of `Double.compare(a, b)` rather than hand-rolling `a - b`-style arithmetic: for `double`/`float`, subtraction can produce subtly wrong results around `NaN` and near-equal floating point values, and for `int`, `a - b` risks silent integer overflow for extreme values -- the static `compare()` helpers on the wrapper classes (`Integer.compare`, `Double.compare`, etc.) handle these edge cases correctly and are the idiomatic choice.

`compareTo()` (and `compare()`) have to satisfy a strict, transitive contract for sorting algorithms and sorted collections (`TreeSet`, `TreeMap`) to behave correctly: if `a.compareTo(b)` is negative and `b.compareTo(c)` is negative, then `a.compareTo(c)` must also be negative -- the ordering has to be globally consistent, not just locally sensible for any one pair examined in isolation. A common way to violate this by accident is comparing by a field using unsafe subtraction.

```java
public class BuggyProduct implements Comparable<BuggyProduct> {
    int priceInCents;

    @Override
    public int compareTo(BuggyProduct other) {
        return priceInCents - other.priceInCents;   // risks silent int overflow!
    }
}
```

For most everyday values this appears to work correctly in casual testing, but if `priceInCents` holds values near the extremes of `int`'s range, the true mathematical difference can exceed what an `int` can represent, and Java's `int` arithmetic silently wraps around (overflows) rather than throwing any error -- which can flip the sign of the result entirely, corrupting the sort for exactly those extreme-value pairs. A separate, sharper pitfall worth knowing: it's strongly recommended, though not strictly enforced by the compiler, that `compareTo()`'s notion of 'equal' (returning zero) be consistent with `equals()` -- `TreeSet` and `TreeMap`, specifically, use `compareTo()` (or a supplied `Comparator`) as their *sole* notion of equality internally, meaning two objects that are 'equal' by `compareTo()` but not actually `.equals()` will be silently treated as duplicates and only one will be kept, which can be a surprising, hard-to-diagnose difference from a `HashSet`, which uses `equals()`/`hashCode()` instead.

